---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import DocPage from '../../../components/DocPage.astro';
import { site } from '../../../data/site.js';

const description =
    'Migrate a PrimeVue 4 project to OpenVue with one command: npx @openvue/migrate. Every package it renames, what stays the same, and the icons, Volt and v3 paths.';

const url = `${site.url}/projects/openvue/migrate`;

const contents = [
    { id: 'before-you-start', text: 'Before you start' },
    { id: 'run-the-migration', text: 'Run the migration' },
    { id: 'what-it-changes', text: 'What it changes' },
    { id: 'package-map', text: 'Package map' },
    { id: 'what-stays-the-same', text: 'What stays the same' },
    { id: 'icons', text: 'Icons' },
    { id: 'volt', text: 'Coming from Volt' },
    { id: 'try-first', text: 'Try it without changing code' },
    { id: 'older-versions', text: 'PrimeVue 3 and earlier' }
];

const schema = [
    {
        '@context': 'https://schema.org',
        '@type': 'HowTo',
        '@id': `${url}#howto`,
        name: 'How to migrate from PrimeVue to OpenVue',
        description,
        totalTime: 'PT10M',
        tool: [{ '@type': 'HowToTool', name: '@openvue/migrate' }],
        step: [
            {
                '@type': 'HowToStep',
                name: 'Check the PrimeVue version',
                text: 'Make sure the project is on PrimeVue 4.x and the git working tree is clean.',
                url: `${url}#before-you-start`
            },
            {
                '@type': 'HowToStep',
                name: 'Run the codemod',
                text: 'Run npx @openvue/migrate at the project root, review the plan it prints, and choose a full migration.',
                url: `${url}#run-the-migration`
            },
            {
                '@type': 'HowToStep',
                name: 'Review the diff',
                text: 'Review the single diff it leaves and fix any PrimeVue references it lists as remaining.',
                url: `${url}#what-it-changes`
            },
            {
                '@type': 'HowToStep',
                name: 'Swap the icon stylesheet',
                text: "Replace the primeicons import with '@openvue/openicons/primeicons.css' so existing pi pi-* classes keep rendering.",
                url: `${url}#icons`
            }
        ]
    }
];

const packageMap = [
    { from: 'primevue, primevue/*', to: 'openvue, openvue/*' },
    {
        from: '@primevue/core, forms, icons, themes, nuxt-module, auto-import-resolver, metadata, mcp',
        to: 'Same names under @openvue/*'
    },
    { from: '@primeuix/themes, @primeuix/themes/*', to: '@openvue/themes, @openvue/themes/*' },
    { from: '@primeuix/styled, utils, styles, forms, mcp', to: 'Same names under @openuxkit/*' }
];
---

<Base
    title="Migrate from PrimeVue to OpenVue"
    description={description}
    schema={schema}
    image="/og/openvue/migrate.png"
    breadcrumbs={[
        { name: 'Projects', path: '/projects' },
        { name: 'OpenVue', path: '/projects/openvue' },
        { name: 'Migrate from PrimeVue', path: '/projects/openvue/migrate' }
    ]}
>
    <DocPage
        title="Migrate from PrimeVue"
        label="Migration guide"
        lede="OpenVue keeps PrimeVue 4's public API unchanged, so migrating is a package rename. One command does it and leaves a single diff to review."
        contents={contents}
    >
        <pre><code>npx @openvue/migrate</code></pre>
        <p>
            That is the whole migration for most projects. The rest of this page is what the command
            does, so you know what you are reviewing, and the few cases it leaves to you.
        </p>

        <h2 id="before-you-start">Before you start</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>PrimeVue 4.x.</strong> OpenVue forked from 4.5.5. The tool detects the installed
                major version and stops without changing anything if it is not 4.x.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Node.js 18 or newer.</strong></li>
            <li>
                <strong>A clean git working tree.</strong> Then the migration is one commit you can read
                and revert. Interactively the tool only asks if the tree is dirty; in CI or with a mode
                flag it stops unless you pass <code>--force</code>.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="run-the-migration">Run the migration</h2>
        <p>
            From the project root (for a monorepo, the workspace root), run the command above. It
            prints a plan first: the detected package manager, the PrimeVue version, the files and
            references it will change, and your git status. Then it asks how to proceed:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Full migration</strong> rewrites files and runs your package manager's install,
                so the project builds straight away.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Files only</strong> rewrites files and dependencies and leaves
                <code>node_modules</code> alone for you to install.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Dry run</strong> shows everything and writes nothing.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>To run it without the prompt, in CI for example, pass the mode:</p>
        <pre><code>npx @openvue/migrate --dry
npx @openvue/migrate --mode files-only
npx @openvue/migrate --yes</code></pre>
        <p>
            If OpenVue is already installed, the tool switches to sources-only mode: it rewrites
            imports and leaves dependencies and <code>node_modules</code> untouched.
        </p>

        <h2 id="what-it-changes">What it changes</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <strong>Dependencies</strong> in every <code>package.json</code>, including
                <code>overrides</code>, <code>resolutions</code>, <code>pnpm.overrides</code> and
                <code>pnpm-workspace.yaml</code> catalogs. <code>workspace:</code>,
                <code>catalog:</code> and <code>npm:</code> values are preserved.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Imports</strong> in <code>.js</code>, <code>.ts</code>, <code>.vue</code>,
                <code>.jsx</code>, <code>.tsx</code>, <code>.astro</code> and <code>.mdx</code> files:
                static and dynamic imports, <code>require()</code>, Nuxt <code>modules</code>, Vite
                <code>optimizeDeps</code> and generated <code>components.d.ts</code>. A bare
                <code>'primevue'</code> string is only rewritten where it is an import, so runtime data
                such as <code>provider: 'primevue'</code> is left alone.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>An audit</strong> of what is left. Interpolated imports, CSS
                <code>@import</code>s, CDN URLs and tsconfig <code>paths</code> it could not rewrite
                safely are listed with file and line, so it never reports success while a reference
                remains.
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            <code>openvue</code> and <code>@openvue/*</code> are pinned to the exact version the tool
            ships with, so a migration is reproducible. <code>@openuxkit/*</code> gets a caret range,
            because it versions independently. Widen either afterwards if you prefer.
        </p>

        <h2 id="package-map">Package map</h2>
        <div class="table-scroll">
            <table class="spec">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">PrimeVue</th>
                        <th scope="col">OpenVue</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    {
                        packageMap.map((row) => (
                            <tr>
                                <td class="spec-package">
                                    <code>{row.from}</code>
                                </td>
                                <td>
                                    <code>{row.to}</code>
                                </td>
                            </tr>
                        ))
                    }
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="what-stays-the-same">What stays the same</h2>
        <p>
            Component props, slots and events match PrimeVue 4.x. So do the names your code already
            uses: <code>usePrimeVue</code>, <code>PrimeVueResolver</code>, the <code>p-</code> CSS
            classes, pass-through options, and the <code>primevue</code> key in
            <code>nuxt.config</code>, which the OpenVue Nuxt module kept on purpose.
        </p>
        <p>
            Three packages are deliberately left untouched: <code>primeicons</code>,
            <code>primeflex</code> and <code>tailwindcss-primeui</code>. The icons have a maintained
            replacement, covered next.
        </p>

        <h2 id="icons">Icons</h2>
        <p>
            <a href="/projects/openicons">OpenIcons</a> continues PrimeIcons. Swap the package and the
            stylesheet, and every existing <code>pi pi-*</code> class keeps rendering through a
            compatibility layer:
        </p>
        <pre><code>npm uninstall primeicons
npm install @openvue/openicons</code></pre>
        <pre class="code-diff"><code><span class="diff-del">- import 'primeicons/primeicons.css';</span><span class="diff-add">+ import '@openvue/openicons/primeicons.css';</span></code></pre>
        <p>
            Move to the new <code>oi oi-*</code> classes at your own pace by importing
            <code>@openvue/openicons/openicons.css</code>; both stylesheets can be loaded at once. The
            one icon removed rather than renamed is <code>pi-prime</code>, the PrimeTek logo mark.
        </p>

        <h2 id="volt">Coming from Volt</h2>
        <p>
            If the project uses PrimeVue Volt components, run the migration first, then replace them
            with <a href="/projects/openvolt">OpenVolt</a>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>npx @openvue/migrate
npx openvolt-vue add all --overwrite</code></pre>
        <p>
            Leave out <code>--overwrite</code> to keep the components you have customised; only the
            untouched ones are replaced.
        </p>

        <h2 id="try-first">Try it without changing code</h2>
        <p>
            To evaluate OpenVue before committing to the rename, alias it in place of PrimeVue. No
            source file changes:
        </p>
        <pre><code>{`// package.json (npm and bun; yarn uses "resolutions")
{
  "overrides": {
    "primevue": "npm:openvue@1.0.0"
  }
}`}</code></pre>
        <p>
            On pnpm 10.5 and newer the override belongs in <code>pnpm-workspace.yaml</code> under
            <code>overrides</code>. Once you decide to stay, run <code>npx @openvue/migrate</code> for
            the real rename.
        </p>

        <h2 id="older-versions">PrimeVue 3 and earlier</h2>
        <p>
            Upgrade to PrimeVue 4 first with the <a
                href="https://openvue.dev/guides/migration/v4/"
                rel="noopener">v4 migration guide</a
            >, then run <code>npx @openvue/migrate</code>. The tool will not run on a project that is
            not on 4.x.
        </p>
        <p>
            Stuck on something the tool did not handle? <a
                href="https://github.com/openvi-foundation/openvue/issues/new/choose"
                rel="noopener">Open an issue</a
            > or ask in <a href={site.discord} rel="noopener">Discord</a>. Migration gaps are bugs, and
            we fix them.
        </p>
    </DocPage>
</Base>
